        "problem": "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}+27>0.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}+27 & >0 \\\\ 3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x} & >-27 \\\\ {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x} & >-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $\\frac{15}{4}>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}$ is greater than $-9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}>-9\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers.",
